## The idea

Two trees are the same if they match **node by node**: both missing, or both present with equal values and matching children. Walk them together, comparing pairs. An explicit stack does the walk without recursion, so a very deep tree cannot overflow.

## Step by step

1. Push the pair (p, q).
2. Pop a pair: both null → fine, continue.
3. One null, or different values → return false.
4. Otherwise push (left, left) and (right, right).
5. Stack empty → true.

## Worked example

**Input:** p = [1,2,3], q = [1,2,3]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| pop (1,1) | equal → push (2,2), (3,3) |
| pop (3,3) | equal → push their null children |
| pop (2,2) | equal |
| nulls | skip |

**Result:** true

## Cost

O(n) time; O(h) memory for the stack.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// TreeNode is provided by the starter (Models.cs): int Value; TreeNode Left, Right.

// LeetCode 100 — are two trees the same shape with the same values?
public static class SameTree {
    public static bool Check(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        // Compare node pairs with an explicit stack: no recursion, so a very deep tree cannot overflow.
        var stack = new Stack<(TreeNode, TreeNode)>();
        stack.Push((p, q));
        while (stack.Count > 0) {
            var (x, y) = stack.Pop();
            if (x == null && y == null) continue;        // both missing here: still the same
            if (x == null || y == null) return false;    // one missing: different shape
            if (x.Value != y.Value) return false;
            stack.Push((x.Left, y.Left));
            stack.Push((x.Right, y.Right));
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Is the in-order sequence enough?**

No — [1,2] (left child) and [1,null,2] (right child) have the same in-order with null markers removed.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) time; O(h) memory for the stack.

**Q: Recursive or iterative?**

Recursive is clearer; iterative survives a 100,000-deep chain. Say which you chose and why.

**Q: Shared state?**

Read-only trees.

**Q: Compare two huge trees often.**

Store a Merkle hash per node (hash of value + children hashes); equal roots are equal trees in O(1).

**Q: Related?**

Subtree of another tree (LeetCode 572) — the same hash idea finds it in O(n).

